@h1 How to add a node or renderer h1@

A practical guide to extending Wit's vocabulary and emitting new output formats.
For developers.

@h2 Three ways to get a new node h2@

@ul
@li @strong A user definition strong@ — no code. Write @@@code #name … name# code@@@
and use it. This is the right answer for almost every "new node." li@
@li @strong A core-vocabulary addition strong@ — code in the runtime and every
renderer. Reserve this for shapes that belong to the language itself. li@
@li @strong The opaque pass-through strong@ — @@@code @node(type …) code@@@ carries
its parameters straight to the renderer, which dispatches on the @code type code@
convention. This is the seam for renderer-specific widgets. li@
ul@

@h2 Adding to the core vocabulary h2@

A core node is a commitment: every renderer must handle it. The cadence is
test-first.

@ul
@li Register the name in @code core-vocab.ts code@ so the resolver stops requiring
a definition for it. li@
@li Add a handler in each renderer — the HTML and Markdown renderers both need a
branch. li@
@li Add a fixture under the core-vocab fixtures, with its snapshot, @strong before strong@
the change — the fixtures are the executable spec, and a core node without one
breaks that contract. li@
ul@

@h2 Writing a custom renderer h2@

Walk the expanded AST and dispatch on each node's @code kind code@. The kinds form
a stable, serializable, exhaustive union — you can enumerate them with
@code wit tour code@. A minimal skeleton switches on @code kind code@, recurses
into children, and handles a node use by its name.

@aside(class note) 💡 The full walkthrough, with a verified plain-text renderer,
is @a |href /docs/recipes/custom-renderer/| Write a custom renderer a@. This page
is the map; that recipe is the territory. aside@

@h2 The opaque escape valve h2@

When you need a renderer-specific widget — a chart, a map, a game-state panel —
@@@code @node(type …) code@@@ passes its parameters through untouched. The
resolver skips binding lookup and the expander emits the node intact, so your
renderer sees every parameter and can build whatever it likes. User-defined
wrappers compose over it cleanly, so authors get a friendly name and the renderer
gets the raw payload.

@h2 Wiring it in h2@

Whether you drive the CLI or your own program, the shape is the same:
@code parse code@, @code resolve code@, @code expand code@, then your renderer. A
document that loads external data adds @code loadExternalData code@ before
@code resolve code@.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li @strong A core node without a fixture. strong@ It breaks the executable-spec
contract — add the fixture and snapshot first. li@
@li @strong Lifting stale prose. strong@ Older reference files predate recent
features; verify any snippet against current renderer code and a real build
before copying it. li@
ul@

